\chapter{Tutorial: Screen a search in tiers}
\label{ch:tutorial-tiered-search}

[[tiered-search]] follows the export tutorial
(\cref{ch:tutorial-enrich-verify-export}) and takes up a problem the
earlier lessons hide by keeping their searches small: a real search
returns more records than anyone screens one by one.
Scholar's answer is to screen the head of the answers first (a
\emph{tier}) and to estimate what the rest holds from a seeded random
sample of it.
The object of learning is that answer and its limits.
Four aspects are critical, and each step below opens one of them:
\begin{description}
\item[A place belongs to an answer] A tier selected by place is the
  union of the heads of several answers, so two wordings of one
  question give a tier of at most twice the papers of one, and fewer
  when their heads overlap.
\item[Only a ranked answer has a head] A place counts only in an
  answer ordered by relevance; being first in an answer of no known
  order says nothing about a record.
\item[A tier is recomputed from the pending papers] What has been
  decided falls out of both the tier and the rest, so the order of
  screening and sampling matters.
\item[The rest is estimated, not screened] A sample of the rest gives
  a share with an interval, and nothing at all until it is screened;
  the report's sentence says so in Scholar's own words.
\end{description}

The arc keeps one session, [[tiers]], and one rule, [[--top 3]],
invariant throughout:
\begin{enumerate}
\item search one question in two wordings into the session
  (steps~1--2),
\item read what the session records about each answer (step~3),
\item predict, then see, how many papers the tier takes (steps~4--5),
\item apply the rule to an answer in no known order (step~6),
\item screen the tier (step~7),
\item draw a sample of the rest and read the report before and after
  screening it (steps~8--10),
\item say what a method section can claim from all this (step~11).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{What the keyless providers allow.}
The lesson uses OpenAlex only, since the tutorial shell keeps its
rate-limit memory in the sandbox and a lesson must run without keys
(\cref{ch:tutorial-first-search}).
That costs one contrast: the keyless providers OpenAlex, arXiv and
HAL all record their answers as ordered by relevance (each provider's
[[ANSWER_ORDER]], \cref{sec:answer-orders}), and the keyless provider
that records no order, DBLP, answers scripts with an anti-bot page
(\cref{sec:dblp-transient-failures}).
The learner can therefore not \emph{see} an answer in no known order
beside a ranked one.
Step~6 builds that contrast as a question instead, after step~5 has
shown the tier's own count of papers \enquote{placed only in answers
not ordered by relevance}, so the question asks the learner to apply a
line they have read rather than to guess.

